WEBVTT

c01
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.475
This is the HR case from Lesson 1: a services company with about 400 employees.

c02
00:00:09.655 --> 00:00:16.539
HR gets about 90 requests a month, most of them repeats: vacation days, pension, expenses, overtime.

c03
00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:20.530
They arrive by WhatsApp, by email, and in person, with no system.

c04
00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.455
Nobody knows how many need human judgment.

c05
00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:26.180
The tempting first move is a chatbot.

c06
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.705
Not yet. First we map, AS-IS: how it actually happens on a normal day.

c07
00:00:30.885 --> 00:00:32.627
No fixing, no AI.

c08
00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:39.149
Field 1 is the process name, plain and specific: answering employees' repeat questions to HR.

c09
00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:44.677
Field 2 is the trigger, the concrete thing that starts the process: a message, a form, a date.

c10
00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:46.599
Before I fill it in, you choose.

c11
00:00:48.249 --> 00:00:51.889
All three channels go on the map, because all three really happen.

c12
00:00:52.069 --> 00:00:54.686
Where a process starts and ends is a decision.

c13
00:00:54.866 --> 00:00:59.189
Without clear boundaries, it swells until it covers the whole organization.

c14
00:00:59.639 --> 00:01:02.491
Field 3 is the steps, each with a role owner.

c15
00:01:02.671 --> 00:01:07.975
Here there are three: receiving the request, checking the topic, then an answer or an escalation.

c16
00:01:08.155 --> 00:01:12.372
Owners are roles, not names, so the map stays true when people change.

c17
00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:16.804
Each role gets its own lane, so you can always see who is holding the work.

c18
00:01:16.984 --> 00:01:20.966
Field 4 asks where responsibility passes from one role to another.

c19
00:01:22.616 --> 00:01:27.537
From HR staff to the HR manager, when a question goes beyond known policy.

c20
00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:33.597
That's the most dangerous moment in a process: the staffer assumes the manager knows what happened before.

c21
00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:39.765
A good map marks each handoff: from whom, to whom, what passes, and what if the receiver is unavailable.

c22
00:01:39.945 --> 00:01:42.177
Here, there's no single channel for it.

c23
00:01:42.627 --> 00:01:48.295
Field 5 is knowledge sources: the vacation, pension, expense, and overtime policies.

c24
00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:55.251
Each gets an owner, here the HR manager, and a sensitivity tag: public, internal, confidential, or restricted.

c25
00:01:55.431 --> 00:02:03.637
These policies are internal. An employee's personal or financial details are confidential, and never go into a public AI tool.

c26
00:02:04.087 --> 00:02:06.299
Field 6 is existing controls.

c27
00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:09.245
Is anything checking the answers before they go out?

c28
00:02:09.425 --> 00:02:10.697
What goes in the map?

c29
00:02:12.347 --> 00:02:15.924
No formal control: it depends on the staffer's own judgment.

c30
00:02:16.104 --> 00:02:19.382
That's a valid answer, and exactly what mapping reveals.

c31
00:02:19.832 --> 00:02:22.683
Field 7 is gaps, and here you can write unclear.

c32
00:02:22.863 --> 00:02:27.528
Nobody knows how many wrong answers were given, and there's no single handoff channel.

c33
00:02:27.708 --> 00:02:29.535
That's a diagnostic result.

c34
00:02:29.985 --> 00:02:32.175
Field 8 is the success metric.

c35
00:02:32.355 --> 00:02:37.340
Today, about 35 percent of the HR team's time goes to repeat questions.

c36
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.859
Target: 20 percent within three months.

c37
00:02:40.039 --> 00:02:41.675
A number you can check later.

c38
00:02:41.855 --> 00:02:44.023
Next, you fill Field 5 yourself.
